		<category><![CDATA[WWF vs. WCW]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.wrestleview.com/?p=398461</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>WWF Monday Night Raw ResultsNovember 25, 1996 (Taped November 18)New Haven, Connecticut (New Haven Coliseum)Commentary: Vince McMahon, Jerry&#8230;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/398461-wwf-raw-results-november-25-1996-new-haven/">WWF Raw Results &#8211; 11/25/96 (Bret &#8220;Hitman&#8221; Hart vs. Owen Hart, Rocky Maivia&#8217;s first Raw match)</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com">WWE News, WWE Results, AEW News, AEW Results</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full is-resized"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="750" height="382" src="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-12.png" alt="" class="wp-image-398462" style="aspect-ratio:1.9634483060875663;width:192px;height:auto" srcset="https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-12.png 750w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-12-600x306.png 600w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-12-379x193.png 379w, https://www.wrestleview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-12-550x280.png 550w" sizes="(max-width: 750px) 100vw, 750px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Monday Night Raw Results<br>November 25, 1996 (Taped November 18)<br>New Haven, Connecticut (New Haven Coliseum)<br>Commentary: Vince McMahon, Jerry “The King” Lawler, and Jim Ross<br>Results by: Mike Tedesco of Wrestleview.com</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fireworks go off as Bret “Hitman” Hart makes his way to the ring for his first action on Monday Night Raw since March. Hart goes outside the ring and gives his sunglasses to a young lady.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Shawn Michaels is shown being led into José Lothario&#8217;s living room by Lothario&#8217;s wife, Jean. Lothario is seated on the couch with his grandchildren. We’ll hear from them later tonight.</p>



<h3 id="bret-hitman-hart-vs-wwf-tag-team-champion-owen-hart-w-clarence-mason" class="wp-block-heading">Bret “Hitman” Hart vs. WWF Tag Team Champion Owen Hart w/ Clarence Mason</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Billy Silverman</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They lock up, and Bret powers Owen into the corner before giving him a rough break. Owen shouts for Bret to get out of his face. They circle the ring and lock up. They tussle against the ropes, looking for an advantage. Bret presses Owen into the corner and backs up. They lock up again, and Owen wrenches Bret’s arm, bringing him down to his knees. Bret rolls through and kips up, but Owen pulls him down by the hair. Bret quickly gets up, wrenches his arm, and pulls Owen down by his hair. They lock up, and Bret hits an arm drag takeover. Bret knees Owen in the arm and wrenches it. Owen twists and kips up before turning the pressure on Bret. Bret sends Owen into the ropes for a back elbow. Bret drops an elbow on Owen’s chest and applies an overhead wristlock. Owen fights up and takes Bret down with a drop toehold, but Bret quickly fires back with a hammerlock. Owen quickly grabs the rope to get Bret off him.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They get into a shoving match before slapping each other. Bret rolls Owen up for a two-count. Bret goes back to the overhead wristlock. Owen fights up, but Bret stays in control of the arm. Owen does a standing switch, but Bret quickly turns it on him. Owen quickly elbows him in the face and hits the ropes, but Bret hits a hip toss, followed by a clothesline. Bret headbutts Owen and goes back to the arm. Owen backs Bret into the corner. Bret reverses a whip to the opposite corner and hits a back body drop. Bret punches Owen and schoolboys him for a two-count.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment:</strong> WWF Tag Team Champion The British Bulldog is watching this match on a television.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bret grabs Owen’s arm, but Owen whips him off, drops down, and hits a kitchen sink knee to the midsection. Owen grabs Bret’s legs and stomps his midsection twice. Owen drives Bret’s head into the top turnbuckle and punches him down. Owen clubs Bret in the spine and whips him hard into the turnbuckles. Owen hits Bret with a backbreaker before sending him back into the corner. Owen then applies a camel clutch. Owen shouts that he’s a champion, and Bret is a loser. Bret fights up and punches out before hitting the ropes. Owen drops down and hits a belly-to-belly suplex for a near fall. Owen gets in the referee’s face.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bret fights back at Owen and reverses a whip. Owen flips through a backbreaker attempt. Bret blocks a kick, but Owen floors him with an enzuigiri.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Owen is standing on the top rope, but Bret gets up and slams him down to the canvas. They get into a brawl before Bret headbutts him down. Bret sends Owen into the ropes and punches him in the midsection before hitting a side Russian leg sweep for a near fall. Bret hits a backbreaker and goes to the second rope for a diving elbow. Bret headbutts Owen in the midsection and picks up a near fall. Owen quickly rolls Bret up for a two-count. The kickout sends Owen out of the ring.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Owen gets on the apron and shoulders Bret. Owen goes for a slingshot sunset flip, but Bret rolls through and sets up for a Sharpshooter. All of a sudden, <strong>“Stone Cold” Steve Austin</strong> runs in and smashes Bret&#8217;s spine with a steel chair!</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-disqualification-bret-hitman-hart" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Disqualification: Bret “Hitman” Hart</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Austin stomps on Bret before putting the steel chair around his ankle, à la what he did to Brian Pillman. <strong>The British Bulldog</strong> quickly runs in and pulls the chair off Bret’s leg. Bulldog gets in Austin’s face. Owen grabs the chair and wants it around Bret’s leg. Bulldog shoves Owen a few times to talk some sense into him. Austin then smashes Bulldog in the spine with the chair! Owen gets in Austin’s face before Austin walks off.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258369 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> A strong professional wrestling match between two phenomenal competitors. I don’t think Bret Hart and Owen Hart could have had a bad match if they tried. Even with a lame finish, they still put together a good effort. It wasn’t anything dazzling or on the level of their classic 1994 matches, but it was straight-up quality wrestling.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258370 wp-block-paragraph">I liked the interference from Steve Austin to keep the feud with Bret Hart alive and well. I was also interested in what they’re doing with The British Bulldog and Owen Hart, teasing an issue between the two where Bulldog doesn’t want Owen going too far with family, even if Owen and Bret don’t like each other. I’m interested to see where it goes, if anywhere. They kind of started playing with tension between Owen and Bulldog back in August on the Raw after SummerSlam when they faced each other in the Intercontinental Championship Tournament, but nothing really came of it at the time.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258371 wp-block-paragraph">Also, on a sad note, this is the final televised singles match Bret Hart and Owen Hart will ever have. They’ll have one more singles match in 1997 at a house show in Hamburg, Germany, but this is the last one captured for television. After their classic battles at WrestleMania X and SummerSlam 1994, Bret and Owen left behind a body of work that can still be enjoyed and studied today.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Replay:</strong> Moments ago, “Stone Cold” Steve Austin attacked Bret “Hitman” Hart with a steel chair during his match against Owen Hart. The British Bulldog made the save for Bret and got in Austin’s face. Bulldog and Owen argued a bit before Austin smashed Bulldog in the spine with the steel chair.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Executioner is led to the ring by Paul Bearer. Mankind limps behind them. Jim Ross is no longer on commentary.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Paul Bearer, The Executioner, and Mankind were in the locker room earlier today. Bearer said they’re not concerned about The Undertaker. Mankind cradles the urn as he says, “Have a nice day!”</p>



<figure class="wp-block-embed is-type-video is-provider-youtube wp-block-embed-youtube wp-embed-aspect-16-9 wp-has-aspect-ratio"><div class="wp-block-embed__wrapper">
<iframe loading="lazy" title="The Executioner vs. Freddie Joe Floyd: Raw, November 25, 1996" width="1200" height="675" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/vUhliwtSbFQ?feature=oembed" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen></iframe>
</div></figure>



<h3 id="freddie-joe-floyd-vs-the-executioner-w-paul-bearer-and-mankind" class="wp-block-heading">Freddie Joe Floyd vs. The Executioner w/ Paul Bearer and Mankind</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Jack Doan</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They lock up, and Floyd ducks a right hand before hitting an arm drag. Floyd hits The Executioner with a fireman’s carry takeover. Floyd wrenches the arm, but The Executioner punches and knees Floyd in the face. The Executioner sends him into the ropes and hits an ugly dropkick. The Executioner then clotheslines Floyd over the top rope. The Executioner sends Floyd to the corner and hits a bulldog. The Executioner punches Floyd to the corner.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview:</strong> Dok Hendrix says The British Bulldog and Owen Hart were arguing backstage moments ago. As for “Stone Cold” Steve Austin, he blew Hendrix off and left the arena, but that doesn’t mean he won’t come back.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the ring, The Executioner hits a sloppy back suplex where Floyd lands on him first before hitting the mat. The Executioner sends him to the corner, and Floyd drops to the mat. The Executioner hits a scoop slam before going for an elbow drop, but Floyd moves. Floyd punches away at The Executioner and takes him down with a jumping heel kick for a two-count. They exchange punches before The Executioner knocks him to the corner. The Executioner sends him to the opposite corner and hits a running clothesline. The Executioner repeats the move before throwing him to the center of the ring and hitting another clothesline. The Executioner signals for the end and digs the Asian Spike into his throat. The referee calls for the bell.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-technical-submission-the-executioner" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Technical Submission: The Executioner</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Paul Bearer gets in the ring to pull The Executioner off Freddie Joe Floyd. Mankind is cradling The Executioner’s ax. The Executioner will battle The Undertaker at In Your House in an Armageddon Rules Match.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258387 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> There was just nothing good about that match. “The Executioner” Terry Gordy is so diminished in his skills by this point. The only thing I’m happy about is that he was able to earn a steady paycheck for his family for a little while now. When you see his eyes and the way he moves, it’s obvious this is not the Terry Gordy people remember from his prime. His offense looked really soft and anything but crisp. I’m kind of curious to see the Armageddon Rules match against The Undertaker on pay-per-view to see if they can pull a proverbial rabbit out of the hat because the way Gordy looked in this match (and for pretty much his entire run), I don’t have high hopes for anything else.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258388 wp-block-paragraph">It’s a shame because, in his prime, Terry Gordy was one of the finest professional wrestlers there was. I’ll do a full check on his career at a later date.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Later tonight, Billy Gunn will battle “Wildman” Marc Mero.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Shawn Michaels and José Lothario are in San Antonio, Texas, waiting for their interview. Neither man looks happy.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<h3 id="shawn-michaels-and-jose-lothario-address-survivor-series" class="wp-block-heading">Shawn Michaels and José Lothario address Survivor Series</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Former WWF Champion Shawn Michaels is seated next to José Lothario on a couch in Lothario’s home. Michaels is wearing a bandana, red shirt, black leather vest, and jeans. Vince McMahon asks about Lothario’s health. Lothario says he feels guilty over Shawn Michaels losing the WWF Championship. Michaels taps him on the leg and tells him to move on. Lothario says he checked with the doctors. They want to put a balloon in his heart because it was damaged at the Survivor Series. Lothario will stay home for a bit to make sure he does the right thing.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon asks a somber Michaels about the Royal Rumble press conference and if the boyhood dream has been shattered. Can he put the pieces back together? Will we see that passionate Shawn Michaels again? Michaels says a lot of people have been saying there was an “old” Shawn Michaels compared to what he is now. Michaels doesn’t know what everyone liked or disliked. All he knows is that in the last eight months, he lost his edge. That’s his fault. It’s not Lothario’s fault, and Michaels won’t let him take the blame for that. Michaels won’t make an excuse for what happened at Survivor Series. What’s done is done. Michaels can be the WWF Champion again. This is something that cannot ever be replaced.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Jerry “The King” Lawler says he’s had about as much of this “mealy mouth mush” as he can stomach. Lawler calls Lothario an “old fossil” and says he cost Michaels the WWF Title by being up on the ring. If Lothario’s eyesight is that bad, he needs to get his bifocals changed if he needs to see the action that close. Michaels says Lawler needs to understand one thing. Sid says Lothario put himself in the game, but Lothario has been in the game for thirty years and can stick his nose where he damn well pleases as far as Michaels is concerned. Sid stepped over the line. If Sid can beat him, skill for skill, and match his ability, he’s the one man in the WWF who is man enough to shake his hand. First, he hit a 62-year-old man with a camera. Michaels doesn’t care what he did to him. What he did to Lothario is uncalled for. Sid is who Michaels wants. Michaels doesn’t care where he faces Bret Hart. Sid is who he wants next. In his hometown, he and the people of San Antonio will tear him apart.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon asks if it threw Michaels off his game when the fans were cheering Sid more than him. Michaels says it didn’t throw him off. Michaels has always wanted the fans of the WWF to enjoy themselves. He hopes to provide that enjoyment, but if he can’t, they can get it from somewhere else. Michaels has another agenda, and that’s to get the WWF Championship back. He’d love to have the people behind him, but he won’t beg anybody or anyone at any time. He will be the WWF Champion because he wants to be and Lothario believes in him. The people who are important in his life believe in him. If you’re on his side, fine. If you’re not, fine. McMahon says he seems awfully defensive. Michaels says he’s just being honest.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon asks if we’ll see Shawn Michaels, the risk-taker, the flamboyant superstar. Michaels says he’ll take more risks and be more flamboyant. He’ll do what he wants to do. That’s what got him to be the number one man in the World Wrestling Federation because he used to slap people and kick their teeth in when he felt like it. That’s exactly what he’ll do. If he has to gouge an eye, he’ll do it. If he has to kick you in the teeth, he’ll kick. He’ll do what he wants, when he wants. If someone wants to do something about it, they can try him. As the old saying goes, “No more Mr. Nice Guy.” Michaels can play as dirty as anyone.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">McMahon paints the picture of the Alamodome at the Royal Rumble. Michaels has the option of facing Bret Hart or Sycho Sid, whoever is the champion at that time. Will he once again reign? Michaels says he will. He doesn’t care if it’s Bret, but he’d prefer Sid. Michaels and the people of San Antonio will tear him up. In his hometown, there will be 71,000 chances to see him kick Sid’s 6’9” 300-pound teeth down his throat. It’ll happen, and there’s nothing Sid or anybody else can do about it.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258405 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> Continuing with Vince McMahon’s attempt to rebrand Shawn Michaels a bit last week and give him an edge as a “man’s man,” we get this interview. I suppose they realized, with the reaction at Survivor Series, that the flamboyant, nothing-bothers-me babyface who all the girls love wasn’t going to cut it as they push the product toward edgier content. Michaels was attired differently than we’ve seen him before and angrier than we’ve seen him in a long time. It was a bit of a welcome change, but we’ll see how this character shift is received by the audience that isn’t in San Antonio, Texas. The problem is that they’re trying to do two things at once: make Michaels sympathetic because of what happened to Lothario while also making him seem tougher and less polished than before.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Segment</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Rocky Maivia is shown warming up as Shawn Michaels concludes his interview.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“I know you want me.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sunny makes her way to the ring. She goes over to the commentary table and dances with Vince McMahon for a moment. Jerry “The King” Lawler isn’t happy that she’s sitting next to McMahon. Sunny says she likes McMahon’s electric blue. Maybe she’ll get a raise. McMahon puts his hands up and says they’ll be right back.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Milton Bradley Karate Fighters Holiday Tournament:</strong> Jerry “The King” Lawler and Todd Pettengill welcome us back to the tournament where we’ll see some Semifinal action. It’s Sable vs. Sunny. Sable says Tiger Ninja will make your day anything but “sunny.” Sunny says, &#8220;Whatever Sunny wants, Sunny gets.&#8221; Sunny and Sable battle with Sunny coming out on top. The referee then sees that Sunny stuck her Karate Fighter onto the platform with bubblegum and disqualifies her. Sunny isn’t happy and punches the referee.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Salvatore Sincere is in the ring, telling the crowd he loves them. Sunny is on commentary and doesn’t look impressed.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Captain Lou Albano says Rocky Maivia has the potential to be the biggest star in the Maivia family. Don Muraco said Maivia has everything it takes to be a star.</p>



<h3 id="salvatore-sincere-vs-rocky-maivia" class="wp-block-heading">Salvatore Sincere vs. Rocky Maivia</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Billy Silverman</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They circle the ring and lock up. Sincere powers Maivia into the corner and kisses him on both cheeks. The referee holds Maivia back from attacking, and Sincere puts himself through the ropes to keep him back.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview:</strong> Dok Hendrix has an update on Bret Hart. Hart asked that cameras not be brought into his locker room because he’s disoriented. Hart has one major obstacle in the WWF, and it’s not the title match against Sycho Sid. It’s “Stone Cold” Steve Austin. Austin said as long as he and Hart are in the WWF, it’ll never be over.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back to the action, they lock up, and Sincere hooks a side headlock. Maivia pushes him off, but Sincere shoulder tackles him. Maivia kips up and leapfrogs him twice before hitting a dropkick. Maivia hits an arm drag before sending him into the ropes for another. Maivia applies an armbar. Sunny is smitten with Maivia on commentary. Sincere fights up, but Maivia wrenches the arm and elbows him. Sincere whips him off, but Maivia avoids a slam. Maivia goes for a roll-up, but Sincere holds the ropes. Maivia charges, but he flies through the ropes when Sincere moves. Sincere taunts the crowd before hitting Maivia with a baseball slide.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Maivia gets on the apron, so Sincere tries to bring him into the ring the hard way. Maivia reverses it on him and sends Sincere over the top rope to the floor. Sincere snaps Maivia off the top rope and chops him. Maivia reverses a whip, and they each counter a hip toss. Sincere then hits Maivia with a sidewalk slam. Jerry “The King” Lawler makes fun of Maivia’s chia pet hairstyle. Sincere drops an elbow and stands him up, chopping him to the corner. Sincere sends Maivia to the corner, but he hits the ring post when Maivia sidesteps him. Maivia hits a scoop slam and signals for the end. Maivia connects with a Shoulderbreaker for the win.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-pinfall-rocky-maivia" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Pinfall: Rocky Maivia</h3>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258426 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> The only thing of major note in this is that this is Rocky Maivia’s first match on Raw and his first televised singles match in general. Other than the hair, he’s got a great look, but he is very rough around the edges. They’re limiting him to a few spots and some basic wrestling holds for now, but he looks like he has the poise and pedigree to make this work. Time will tell. We’ll also see how the fans react to him getting talked up big time on commentary while not doing a whole heck of a lot in the ring. It still boggles my mind that, of all the finishers they could have given him, he has the Shoulderbreaker as a finish. So random and unexciting. Even something simple with more impact would have been better than this.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">“Wildman” Marc Mero and Sable are backstage. Vince McMahon mentions that Hunter Hearst Helmsley said he’d be on commentary for Mero’s match against Billy Gunn. Mero says he’s got some unfinished business with Helmsley. It’ll be tough to stay focused with him at the commentary table, but if he leaves that table, it’ll be the biggest mistake he could make. After the match, he’ll punch Helmsley in his big nose. It’s time to get wild!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Backstage Interview</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Dok Hendrix has an update on The British Bulldog and Owen Hart. Hendrix says things have calmed down, and they’re focused on retaining the WWF Tag Team Titles on December 15. Hendrix heard The Bulldog tell Hart, in no uncertain terms, that he would get a piece of “Stone Cold” Steve Austin.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WWF Intercontinental Champion Hunter Hearst Helmsley is on commentary for the next match.</p>



<h3 id="wildman-marc-mero-w-sable-vs-billy-gunn" class="wp-block-heading">“Wildman” Marc Mero w/ Sable vs. Billy Gunn</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Referee:</strong> Mike Chioda</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gunn charges down to the ring and attacks Mero from behind. Gunn punches Mero and sends him into the ropes for a back elbow. Gunn sends Mero into the ropes, but Mero hits him with a head-scissor takeover, followed by a back body drop. Mero then clotheslines Gunn over the top rope. Mero hits the ropes and feigns a plancha before coming off the apron with a sledge to the spine. Hunter Hearst Helmsley taunts him from commentary. Gunn grabs Mero from behind, but Mero punches him back and puts him in the ring. Mero hits a slingshot leg drop for a two-count. Mero bounces Gunn off the top turnbuckle and punches him away before doing the ten-punch combo. Gunn reverses a whip to the corner, but Mero boots him back. Mero goes to the top rope and hits a Merosault for a near fall.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mero stands Gunn up and jabs away at him. Mero sends him into the ropes, but Gunn catches him with a leg drop bulldog. Gunn stomps Mero in the head and bounces him off the top turnbuckle. Gunn stomps him in the corner, then pulls him into the center of the ring and hits a dropkick. Mero fights back, but Gunn powers him into the corner. Gunn hits a snapmare and applies a rear chin lock. Gunn puts his feet on the ropes for extra leverage. Helmsley talks about using people to get ahead. Mero fights up and hits the ropes, but Gunn dropkicks him in the knee. Gunn punches away at Mero and covers for a two-count. Gunn repeats it twice for the same result. Gunn stomps Mero and drops a knee for a two-count. Gunn sends Mero into the ropes, but Mero catches him with an inside cradle for a two-count. Gunn pops up and clotheslines Mero down. Gunn pulls Mero up and drops him with a neckbreaker for a two-count. Gunn chokes Mero before the referee stops him. Gunn stands Mero up and hits a swinging neckbreaker before mocking Mero’s taunt. Gunn heads to the top rope, but Mero hits the ropes to crotch him up there.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">-Commercial Break-</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Video:</strong> Milton Bradley’s Karate Fighters presents In Your House: It’s Time, live on December 15 from West Palm Beach, Florida.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back from the break, Mero hits a super hurricanrana for a near fall. Helmsley leaves commentary to approach Sable. Mero bounces Gunn off the top rope and hits a Samoan Drop. Helmsley approaches Sable, who starts to cry. Mero runs outside and threatens Helmsley. Mero blocks an attack and punches Helmsley to the ground. Gunn attacks Mero from behind and gets him in the ring. Helmsley enters the ring, and the referee calls the match off.</p>



<h3 id="winner-by-disqualification-wildman-marc-mero" class="wp-block-heading">Winner by Disqualification: “Wildman” Marc Mero</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Gunn holds Mero up for Helmsley to punch. <strong>Jake “The Snake” Roberts</strong> runs down for the save. Roberts and Mero clear the ring of Helmsley and Gunn.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258447 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mike’s Thoughts:</strong> That was a dreadfully boring match with Marc Mero and Billy Gunn. While Mero seems to be growing more comfortable doing his high-risk style in a WWF ring, his character is really lacking. He’s kind of falling into the same issue Billy Gunn had a few months ago. Billy was stuck in a tag team with Bart Gunn that was long past its expiration date, and they were managed by Sunny, who outshone them in every segment and match. Now Mero is losing the audience’s interest to his valet and real-life wife, Sable.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258448 wp-block-paragraph">That said, it’s not like they’ve given Mero a ton to work with. He had a brief run with the Intercontinental Championship before being swerved by Mr. Perfect, who is no longer with the company, so that storyline can’t pay off. Before that, he was having mediocre matches and giving a lot of offense to jobbers like The Goon and T.L. Hopper. He can’t even decisively beat a guy who spent the last three years in a tag team. For a wrestler who received the WWF’s first guaranteed contract at a number that legitimately angered Steve Austin and Mankind behind the scenes, you’d think they&#8217;d give him better material. Then again, when he opens his mouth, you can see why there is only so far they can go with him.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258449 wp-block-paragraph">Making matters worse, this match was apparently so bad the first time around that they had to re-tape it. Imagine needing a do-over and still ending up with this. A really bad way to close the show. Matches like this aren’t going to cut it in a wrestling war.</p>



<hr class="wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity"/>



<h3 id="quick-match-results" class="wp-block-heading">Quick Match Results</h3>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258453 wp-block-paragraph">&#8212; Bret “Hitman” Hart def. WWF Tag Team Champion Owen Hart via DQ<br>&#8212; The Executioner def. Freddie Joe Floyd via Technical Submission<br>&#8212; Rocky Maivia def. Salvatore Sincere<br>&#8212; “Wildman” Marc Mero def. Billy Gunn via DQ</p>



<h3 id="mikes-wrap-up-what-we-learned-from-raw" class="wp-block-heading">Mike’s Wrap-Up: What We Learned from Raw</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This was a pretty flat episode of Raw coming off a much stronger Survivor Series and last week’s live post-pay-per-view episode. Bret Hart vs. Owen Hart gave the show a strong opening match, and Steve Austin&#8217;s interference kept the Bret/Austin issue alive, but the rest of the night felt like a taped show trying to fill time as the company figures out its next direction.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The most interesting character development came from Shawn Michaels, who appeared from José Lothario’s home in San Antonio and sounded angrier, more defensive, and less polished than usual. After getting booed at Madison Square Garden, it feels like the WWF knows the old smiling, flamboyant babyface presentation needs some adjusting. Michaels is still being positioned as sympathetic because of what happened to Lothario, but he is also being given more edge as the company itself continues moving in that direction.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The show ended on a rough note with Marc Mero vs. Billy Gunn. Mero is becoming more comfortable with the WWF style, but his character still feels limited, and Sable is already becoming the more interesting part of the act. The match was apparently so bad the first time around that they had to re-tape it, which makes the final product even more discouraging. Imagine needing a do-over and still ending up with this. Matches like this won&#8217;t cut it in a wrestling war.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There was also more business drama surrounding Curt Hennig. According to the Wrestling Observer, Hennig’s situation with the WWF, WCW, and Lloyd’s of London became even messier than it originally appeared. Hennig had reportedly been weighing a large permanent disability settlement, but once the possibility of him returning to the ring became real, Lloyd’s backed away from paying the lump sum. Hennig apparently believed the WWF damaged his settlement, contacted WCW, briefly smoothed things over with Vince McMahon, verbally agreed to a new WWF deal, and then no-showed multiple appearances anyway. Now the WWF believes he breached his contract, and WCW may debut him before that deal expires. For a guy who was just involved in the Hunter Hearst Helmsley and Marc Mero storyline, this is another example of the Monday Night War wrecking creative plans behind the scenes.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">WCW still won the night, making it twenty-two straight weeks for Nitro. Raw had a good opener and some notable storyline movement, but this was not a strong episode. The WWF has interesting pieces on the board, especially Bret Hart, Steve Austin, Shawn Michaels, Sycho Sid, and Rocky Maivia, but this show felt like a reminder that the roster is still thin and the weekly television can fall off quickly when the main event is Marc Mero vs. Billy Gunn.</p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258464 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>WWF Raw (USA Network, taped): 2.11 (Record: 16-38)</strong><br><strong>WCW Nitro (TNT, live): 3.10 (Record: 38-16)</strong></p>



<p class="cnvs-block-core-paragraph-1795726258465 wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Winner: WCW Nitro (22-week streak)</strong></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For those celebrating in the United States, I wish you a very Happy Thanksgiving!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Email – <a href="mailto:mike@wrestleview.com">mike@wrestleview.com</a><br>X – <a href="https://x.com/MikeTedescoWV">@MikeTedescoWV</a></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/retro/398021-wwf-raw-results-november-18-1996-new-haven/">Last week’s Raw</a> | Last week’s Nitro</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Thanks for reading!</p>



