		<item>
		<title>Report: Tammy Sytch files amended motion seeking to get released from prison</title>
		<link>https://www.wrestleview.com/featured-top-story/398458-report-tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-released-from-prison/</link>
					<comments>https://www.wrestleview.com/featured-top-story/398458-report-tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-released-from-prison/#respond</comments>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Jerome Wilen]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 20:12:14 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[Featured Top Story]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[WWE News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[amended motion]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[prison]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[release]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tammy Sytch]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.wrestleview.com/?p=398458</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>PWInsider is reporting WWE Hall of Famer Tammy Sytch has filed an amended motion on November 19, seeking&#8230;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/featured-top-story/398458-report-tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-released-from-prison/">Report: Tammy Sytch files amended motion seeking to get released from prison</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com">WWE News, WWE Results, AEW News, AEW Results</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://pwinsider.com/article/214063/tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-out-of-prison.html?p=1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">PWInsider is reporting</a> WWE Hall of Famer Tammy Sytch has filed an amended motion on November 19, seeking to get released from prison.  Sytch, who is currently serving a 17 1/2 year prison sentence at the Lowell Correctional Institution in Ocala, Florida, is attempting to have her conviction dismissed.  </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sytch was convicted in 2023 over a series of charges stemming from the March 2022 death of 75-year old Julian Lasseter during a traffic incident in Ormond Beach, Florida, including DUI causing death, causing death while operating a vehicle with a suspended or revoked driver’s license, four counts of DUI causing injury to a person, and three counts of DUI causing damage to property.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://pwinsider.com/article/214063/tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-out-of-prison.html?p=1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">PWInsider</a> also notes Sytch indicates the motion was filed in good faith, timely, non-duplicative, and that she understands English and is subject to sanctions (including forfeiture of gain time) if it is frivolous or made in bad faith.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In Sytch&#8217;s motion, she again argues the following (courtesy of <a href="https://pwinsider.com/article/214063/tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-out-of-prison.html?p=1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">PWInsider</a>), and is seeking a review of her conviction, broken down by each claim.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Failure to Investigate Causation</strong>:  Sytch alleged her defense counsel rendered &#8220;ineffective assistance&#8221; by failing to investigate and present evidence concerning Julian Lasseter&#8217;s pre-existing medical conditions, toxicology and &#8220;possible alternative causes of death.&#8221;  The motion states, &#8220;The incident involved a low-speed collision. Defendant had slowed her speed and was moving toward a turnaround lane at the time of the collision.  While the death of the decedent is tragic, the available criminal case materials  indicated that the decedent had no significant physical injuries that clearly explained death from the impact itself. Trial counsel did not conduct an adequate investigation into whether the state could prove that the collision legally caused the decedent&#8217;s death.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sytch&#8217;s motion states that through civil discovery, it was learned Lasseter had a &#8220;substantial and long-standing medical history&#8221; including coronary artery disease, stenosis and other serious cardiovascular and internal medical conditions.  It was further noted toxicology records indicated Lasseter had Adderall and Cialis in his system and Lasseter suffered fractured ribs which was consistent with CPR having been administered at the scene.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sytch also argues the fact that, &#8220;These records raise a material medical question whether emergency life-saving measures, pre-existing vascular disease, toxicology findings, a spontaneous medical event, or another medical circumstance contributed to or caused the aortic injury, hemorrhage, and death.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sytch also argued since her defense didn&#8217;t properly investigate or present a qualified any expert evidence regarding Lasseter&#8217;s medical situation, she was convicted.  She also argued, &#8220;there is a reasonable probability that the trier of fact would have harbored reasonable doubt that Defendant&#8217;s driving legally caused the death; Defendant would have been acquitted of the homicide offense; Defendant would have been convicted of a lesser or non-homicide offense, if supported by the charging document and jury instructions; or Defendant would have received a materially more favorable plea or sentencing outcome. The failure to investigate thus undermines confidence in the outcome of Defendant&#8217;s cause.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Failure to Investigate Conflict:</strong> Sytch argued her defense failed to learn lead Assistant State Attorney, Ashley Terwilliger, who helped prosecute her case was married to physician, Zachary Terwilligee, who declared Julian Lasseter’s death.  Sytch is claiming that a proper defense would have investigated the relationship, whether it should have been disclosed to the defense and the court during her trial and “Whether the relationship created an actual conflict, required disclosure, justified recusal, or required another appropriate safeguard.” </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Omitted Evidence</strong>: Sytch argued that her legal counsel provided “ineffective assistance” by not learning of the relationship between the Terwilligers, further claiming that, “Had counsel exercised reasonable diligence, counsel would have discovered the discrepancy, sought full disclosure, investigated medical causation, and litigated the conflict issue before trial or before Defendant entered any plea. There is a reasonable probability that the outcome would have differed if counsel had uncovered and litigated the omitted information. At a minimum, Defendant would have had a meaningful opportunity to investigate the identity and role of the physician, the prosecution&#8217;s medical-causation evidence, and the prosecutor&#8217;s potential conflict.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>New Evidence</strong>: Sytch argued “The evidence concerning the decedent&#8217;s medical history, toxicology, CPR related trauma, and the prosecutor&#8217;s marital relationship to the physician constitutes newly discovered evidence warranting post-conviction relief. “  She also argued that due to lack of funds, she was unable to have counsel who would have discovered all of these facts that have now been unearthed.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PWInsider also notes that in her Motion, Sytch asked the State of Florida respond, hold an evidentiary hearing, appoint her new legal counsel if they find that hearing is necessary &#8211; “Authorize appropriate discovery or compulsory process concerning relevant medical, toxicology, EMS, hospital, pathology, medical examiner, law-enforcement, and civil discovery records”, vacate her current judgement and sentence, grant her a trial trial or “alternatively grant such other postconviction relief as the Court finds lawful” and ‘grant all further relief necessary to remedy the constitutional violations alleged.”</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Back on October 21, Volusia County Circuit Court Judge Christian Miller filed an interim order dismissing Sytch&#8217;s initial motion, citing it was &#8220;facially insufficient.&#8221; Miller wrote Sytch had failed to comply with the requirements of Florida Rule of Criminal Procedure, which states, &#8220;The motion must be under oath stating that the defendant has read the motion or that it has been read to him or her, that the defendant understands its content, and that all of the facts stated therein are true and correct&#8221; and that &#8220;The defendant shall either certify that the defendant can understand English or, if the defendant cannot understand English, that the defendant has had the motion translated completely into a language that the defendant understands.&#8221;  Since Sytch&#8217;s Motion was not filed with the proper parameters, the Court denied it.  However, Sytch has tied up the issues with her new motion.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Furthermore, Miller also issued a warning to Sytch that if she would end up being successful in having her original negotiated plea discarded, the State of Florida would no longer be bound by it and she could be &#8220;exposed to any legal sentence that could be imposed if she is convicted of the charges.&#8221; This would be in regard to some of the lesser charges which had been dropped, but could be brought back into existence if Sytch stands trial again.  The court noted with all the charges, Sytch could face up to 26 years in prison. incarceration &#8211; which would be longer than the initial 17 years, 6 months she was sentenced to in November Sytch entered a plea of no contest in August 2023.  The judge indicated that if Sytch is able to get out of prison and undergo a new trial, she could end up in a worse position than she is already in.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com/featured-top-story/398458-report-tammy-sytch-files-amended-motion-seeking-to-get-released-from-prison/">Report: Tammy Sytch files amended motion seeking to get released from prison</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.wrestleview.com">WWE News, WWE Results, AEW News, AEW Results</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
